  "live_records": {
    "q-1791": {
      "dom_id": "step-q1791",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "John couldn't recall the serial number on his new bike, but he remembered that it was $4$ digits long, each digit ranged from $0$ to $9$ and that none of the digits were used more than once. How many of these serial numbers are possible?",
      "worked_solution": "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $10$ digits, so $n=10.$ We want to select $4$ of those digits for the serial number, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different $4$-digit serial numbers is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{P}_{4} & =\\frac{10!}{(10-4)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot \\cancel{6!}}{\\cancel{6!}} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =5\\,040.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
